  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chance of showers and thunderstorms to arrive late \nSunday morning and Sunday afternoon.\n\nBroad ridging across the central Plains Sunday, will continue to \nbuild, while a trough digs southeast across Quebec. Between these \ntwo features, a well defined thermal gradient will be across the \nGreat Lakes, where a corresponding warm front will strengthen across \nthe upper Great Lakes into New York Sunday. This being said, strong \nfrontogenetic forcing and ample moisture advection will be overhead \nthe upper Great Lakes Sunday morning through Sunday evening, where \nenough moisture and lift will be overhead the eastern Great Lakes to \nsupport a few showers/scattered thunderstorms by late Sunday \nmorning/early Sunday afternoon.\n \nAs the front sags south evening/night, expect shower and \nthunderstorm activity to linger across WNY and the Genesee Valley \ninto Monday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather starting the new work week will \nlinger through mid-week, bringing increasing humidity and several \nrounds of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nAforementioned strong ridge across the Central Plains early next \nweek (Monday and Tuesday) will extend into the lower Ohio Valley \nwhile, troughing across Quebec will continue across the eastern \nGreat Lakes and Northeast. Meanwhile in the lower levels, a well \ndefined thermal gradient will remain across the Great Lakes towards \nNew York and Pennsylvania through early next week. This boundary \nwill waiver north/south at times as a few shortwave passages round \nthe base of the upper level trough.\n\nWith the forcing of each shortwave along with the moisture \nconvergence along the frontal boundary, expect several rounds of \nshowers and scattered thunderstorms through midweek. Initially, \ninstability will remain limited for the start of the week keeping \nthe severe risk low. However, the pattern will become more conducive \nto strong to severe thunderstorms Wednesday, due to a strong Pacific \nshortwave moving east across southern Canada. A plume of an elevated \nmixed layer is forecast to slide northeastward across the Ohio \nValley and possibly the eastern Great Lakes by Wednesday. As this \nfeature arrives, antecedent conditions will feature a warm and humid \nairmass, thus resulting in the potential for moderate to strong \ninstability as the shortwave passes by to the north. Additionally, \nwith the passing of the shortwave, resultant flow will be modified \nto support increasing shear profiles. This all being said, the \npotential for severe weather Wednesday will continue to be monitored \nin the coming days as the details become more clear.",
